Implementation with priority:
IF Jam THEN state := FAULT;
ELSIF Reset THEN state := IDLE;   Note requirement priority says (2) Reset true -> IDLE (even without Jam because Jam checked first). This clears any state, including MOVING, COMPLETE. Then ELSIF state = FAULT THEN hold; ELSIF Stop THEN IDLE; ELSIF state = MOVING AND AtExit THEN COMPLETE; ELSIF state = IDLE AND Start AND NOT AtExit THEN MOVING; ELSE hold. Outputs after transition.

This satisfies. Need ensure initial outputs false before invocation? Outputs default false. state initialized IDLE. Could explicitly init outputs false? In CODESYS VAR_OUTPUT defaults false. State init required. Initial instance observation maybe simulator checks. Fine.

Potential subtlety P5, when qMoving and AtExit and no Jam/Reset/Stop -> transition COMPLETE, outputs Complete & !Belt. yes.

P8 conditions. qComplete & !Jam & !Reset & !Stop -> Complete & !Belt & !Fault. Our complete holds, yes. qMoving !Jam !Reset !Stop !AtExit -> Belt, yes. qIdle with (!Start | AtExit) and no jam/reset -> no outputs, yes. Stop from !qFault and no Jam Reset -> state IDLE and outputs all false. For qFault=false and state maybe? If Stop and state IDLE, reset to IDLE. Good.
P9 !(Belt & Complete) straightforward; (!(Jam | qFault) -> !Fault): our fault only if Jam or prestate fault. If prestate fault and Jam false, Reset false -> remains fault, allowed. If Reset true, clears. If qFault true and Jam false, formula doesn't demand anything except latched P7/P2. P9 last (qIdle | qFault | Stop | Reset) -> !Complete. Our Complete only poststate COMPLETE. If qIdle, cannot transition to complete; qFault no; Stop forces idle; Reset forces idle. Good.

P7 formal semantics: G(Fault -> (G Fault | (Fault U (!Jam & Reset)))) Hmm if Reset while Jam true, remains fault. Eventually reset with no jam clears. yes.

Could implement CASE. Need compilation support enum DUT in supporting_objects. DUT declaration:
